{
  "arguments": {
    "text": "The difference between a Vec and a slice in Rust:\n\n**Vec** (`std::vec::Vec<T>`):\n- A concrete, growable, owned collection that implements the `std::vec::Vec` struct type\n- Allocates its data on the heap\n- Has a well-defined size and type known at compile time\n- Provides methods like `push`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove_with`, etc.\n- Can be moved, cloned, and implements most Collection traits\n- Examples: `Vec<i32>`, `Vec<String>`, `Vec<Option<T>>`\n\n**Slice** (`[T]`):\n- A generic view into a contiguous sequence of elements\n- Does not own its data (can be shared/borrowed)\n- Can be obtained from a `Vec` using `.as_slice()`, `.as_mut_slice()`, or slicing syntax (`&my_vec[..]`)\n- Has no known length at compile time (just a pointer and length field)\n- Implements the `Slice` abstractions (iterator, indexing, etc.)\n- Can be borrowed from `Vec` or arrays\n- Examples: `&[i32]`, `&mut [i32]`, `&[T]`\n\n**Key differences:**\n1. Ownership: `Vec` owns its data, slices borrow it\n2. Implementation: `Vec` is a concrete type, `slice` is a type alias for `*const T` and a length\n3. Defensibility: `Vec` can store any type `T` that implements `Default` and can be shrunk; slices can only borrow\n4. Usage: `Vec` is used for storage, slices are used for working with contents\n5. Collection trait implementation: `Vec` implements `std::iter::FromIterator<T>` and `std::vec::FromIterator<T>`; slices implement `FromIterator<T>` for conversion\n\n**Example:**\n```rust\nlet mut v = Vec::new();  // Owned, resizable vector\nv.push(1);\n\nlet slice = &v[..];       // Owned, slice of Vec\nlet slice_mut = &mut v[..]; // Mutably borrowed slice\n\n// Vec can grow, slices are fixed size\nlet s: &[i32] = &[1, 2, 3];  // Slice\n```\n\nUnderstanding this distinction helps in writing idiomatic Rust code, especially when working with function parameters, return types, and memory management."
}

{
 "arguments": {
  "text": "A Vec<T> is a growable, heap-allocated vector in Rust that owns its data. A slice (&[T]) is a reference to a contiguous block of T- items.\n\nKey differences:\n\n1. **Ownership**: A Vec<T> owns its data and implements Drop, while a slice is just a reference and doesn't own anything.\n\n2. **Mutability**: A Vec<T> can be mutable, while a slice is immutable by reference (&[T]) or mutable by reference (&mut [T]).\n\n3. **Capacity**: A Vec<T> tracks both length and capacity, allowing dynamic growth. A slice only tracks length.\n\n4. **Use case**: Use Vec when you need dynamically sized collections, use slice when working with borrowed data (arrays, vecs, strings, etc. are all)."
 }

